Background. Let $\mathrm{MS}$ denote the variable-free modal language for metric spaces with the following syntax. Fix a countable set of propositional variables. Formulas are built from propositional variables using Boolean connectives and a family of unary modal operators $\exists^{<a}$ and $\exists^{\le a}$, for every positive rational $a$ (with duals $\forall^{<a}$, $\forall^{\le a}$). An $\mathrm{MS}$-model is a pair $\mathfrak{M}=(X,d,\nu)$ where $(X,d)$ is a metric space and $\nu$ assigns to each propositional variable a subset of $X$. The semantics is defined recursively for $x \in X$ by:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathfrak{M},x \models p$ iff $x \in \nu(p)$, with Boolean cases standard;
\item $\mathfrak{M},x \models \exists^{<a}\varphi$ iff there exists $y \in X$ with $d(x,y)<a$ and $\mathfrak{M},y \models \varphi$;
\item $\mathfrak{M},x \models \exists^{\le a}\varphi$ iff there exists $y \in X$ with $d(x,y)\le a$ and $\mathfrak{M},y \models \varphi$.
\end{itemize}
A formula $\varphi$ is satisfiable over a class $\mathcal{C}$ of metric spaces if there is a model whose underlying space lies in $\mathcal{C}$ and a point in it satisfying $\varphi$.

Problem. For natural fragments of $\mathrm{MS}$ (for example, fragments restricting allowed modal operators, allowed parameters, or modal depth), determine the decidability and computational complexity of the satisfiability problem when the class $\mathcal{C}$ consists of bounded subspaces of $n$-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^n$ (with the inherited Euclidean metric), including in particular the unit square $[0,1]\times[0,1] \subset \mathbb{R}^2$.

